Input: {"question": "When did the Women's Strike take place?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00", "start": 0, "end": 50, "text": "the word about the Day Off throughout the country."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00", "start": 51, "end": 202, "text": "The Day Off event organizers got radio stations, television, and newspapers to run stories about gender-based discrimination and lower wages for women."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00", "start": 203, "end": 246, "text": "The event garnered international attention."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00", "start": 247, "end": 368, "text": "On 24 October 1975, Icelandic women did not go to their paid jobs nor did they do any housework or child-rearing at home."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00", "start": 369, "end": 441, "text": "Ninety percent of women took part, including women in rural communities."}, "D1S6": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00", "start": 442, "end": 516, "text": "Fish factories were closed since the factory workers were primarily women."}, "D1S7": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d00", "start": 517, "end": 618, "text": "During the Day Off, 25,000 out of a population of 220,000 people in Iceland gathered in the center of"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d01", "start": 0, "end": 122, "text": "the position for 16 years - years that set Iceland on course to become known as `` the world 's most feminist country '' ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0404-ff7df3fcbbb9:d01", "start": 123, "end": 340, "text": "But Vigdis insists she would never have been president had it not been for the events of one sunny day - 24 October 1975 - when 90 % of women in the country decided to